  "author": "ashtavakra",
  "attributionNote": "Attributed to the sage Ashtavakra, whom the Mahabharata places at the court of king Janaka. The verses were composed centuries later in the non-dual teaching of the Upanishads.",
  "description": "The sage Ashtavakra answers king Janaka in twenty short chapters, and the answer never moves: there is no bondage and no liberation, no seeker and no sought, and the freedom that is wanted is the recognition of what one already is. Practice, purification and effort are put aside less as useless than as belonging to a self that was never bound — a teaching delivered, in the exchange that opens the book, while the king sits on his throne.",
  "historicalContext": "A late Upanishadic text, and among the minor Gitas of the Vedic tradition. It became one of the texts most often carried between India and the West in the twentieth century, and is read in the Advaita and the neo-Vedanta line alike with the Bhagavad Gita, which sets out the same freedom through duty instead.",
